// Lowering: the checked tree to the IR (ir.qk). Every value is a few 8-byte slots (an Int is one, a
// record of three Floats three, an array two: its address and length), so records live in registers, never in
// memory. Loops are fused: map, filter and the like do not build arrays; they add code inside the one loop of their
// source (a range or an array), and sum, fold, any... end it. Small functions and every lambda are inlined.
//
// One Lower value holds the program (read only), the IR made so far and the function being lowered (fn). What C keeps
// in the tree (a name's registers, Binding.regs) is kept here, by binding index (regs, bound...). C's callbacks over a
// loop's elements (Sink) are Sink values: what each does with an element, dispatched by Lower.sink.
//
// Where two of a call's arguments make code, the last one's is made first (the order the code has always had); an
// initializer list ({a, b}) goes from the first.
use ast
use types
use ir
use ownership

// what lowering is asked for: a fuel budget (quark run --fuel), the runtime linked
// (quark object --linked: its loops never collect), the target's calls into C (C structs' layout), and code for the
// debugger (quark debug, --trace, quark dap), its values named by each line's graph key
pub type LowerOptions {
    runtime: Bool = false // the runtime's own code: its allocations are its own calls (no fast path put in)
    fuel: Bool = false
    linked: Bool = false
    arch: Arch = .X64
    sysv: Bool = false
    debug: Bool = false
    debugKeys: [Int: Text] = [:]
}

// the IR, the program's data (texts, constant arrays, interface tables), and where inlined code came from
pub type Lowered { program: IrProgram, data: Bytes, inlines: [InlineCall] }

// inlined code: callee, inlined at line of the code around it (parent, or the function)
pub type InlineCall { parent: Int, callee: Text, line: Int }

